How I Ascended A Ranger, or: Climbing to immortality on the backs of dead
wizards.

A strange game, this one. Level 6 featured the bones of *two* wizards, so I
was quickly able to put myself into a cloak of magic resistance, as well as
collect a sizeable number of spellbooks, rings, and scrolls. One of the
wizards even had a bag of holding, but it was some time before I could even
lift the cursed thing.

Minetown was blessed with a coaligned priest, and several unspectacular
stores. Having picked up more levels than I normally do, I decided against
delving deeper into the mines until I had some better resistances, so I
took a trip to DL7 and cleared Sokoban. Sokoban had a bag of holding (this
is going to be a recurring theme). Returning to the mines with space in the
old inventory, and a big pile of food, I proceeded to find *two* more bags
of holding on my way to the gnome king's cellar. Cellar+1 was a scary
place, featuring a poly trap that put me against a lich and a mindflayer,
but running around screaming with my hands over my head in blind panic, and
occasionally firing arrows eventually brought them down.

All was well until I set foot on DL17, where I discovered the remains of a
black pudding farmer. I appeared in the southwest corner, with 80-100
puddings in the southeast. Southwest also included a large collection of
assorted high level beasties, most telling of which was a Titan. After a
quick change of my undergarments, I quickly burnt the name of some elf on
the stairs with a wand of fire, and made an attempt to take down the Titan
and his minions. This rapidly proved a futile exercise, as the Titan
decided to stand offset from my line of fire and conjure up massed hordes
of foul beasties. When a green slime and a black dragon appeared, I made a
hasty exit up the stairs. So I decided to take Orion up on his offer to
help him out, and quickly traveled to the lair of Scorpius and showed that
overgrown bug the back of my hand. Longbow of Diana in hand, and with a big
pile of blessed poisoned +5 arrows, I went back down to the pudding room
from hell. First priorty was that Titan. He didn't last too long under my
hail of envenomed death, and just as most of the vermin around the stairs
had been cleared off, the giant swarm of puddings from the east arrived.
Out came the trusty Vorpal blade....and then I kind of dozed off.

When I woke up, all the puddings were dead, and I was at the grave of yet a
third wizard, whose equipment far outstripped my own poor collection of
scavenged rags. Also, the 5th bag of holding that I would find. Armed with
phenomenal cosmic power, I blazed down to Gehennom and rapidly cleared it
all the way to the vibrating square (but not before finding 2 *more* bags
of holding...the RNG was mocking my last 4 rangers who all got amulets out
of sokoban and never found any kind of bag whatsoever), leaving the
candelabrum on the spot while I went back up to the pudding room to collect
the bell of opening and my candles. This was rapidly done (the pudding
farmer wizard had kindly left me his quest artifact, which I used to hop up
to *my* Quest level, making this trip far less tedious). Collecting the
necesities out of my storage container, I proceeded down and paid a visit
to my dear friend Rodney. Rodney met the business end of a rubber chicken,
and the book of the dead was in my hands. I was 5 squares from the
vibrating square when I realized that "necesities out of my storage
container" did not include the Bell of Opening, since it didn't fit inside
a storage container.

Oops.

So, back up to my stash I traveled, with Rodney hot on my kidneys. Error
rectified, the sanctum of Moloch was quickly pillaged, and the final
assault on the planes was begun. Earth nearly proved my undoing, as Rodney
was there waiting for me, and in addition to double troubling...twice, he
summoned nasties which included a soldier captain and a cockatrice.
Unfortunately, the captain had gloves. When he picked up and wielded the
rubber chicken, he was quickly shown the business end of a wand of death.

The elemental planes were soon conquered, and a check of the major demons
in the astral plane showed Famine in the middle. I decided to test my
mettle against him, and he proved a meek opponent. The careful application
of a stinking cloud scroll disabled most of the priests in the area, and I
proceeded to the altar, which turned out to be dedicated to the great
Goddess Venus. She was suitably gratified to have the mighty Amulet in her
grasp.


